    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 261246\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n546 AM PDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Dangerous heat expected across the Mojave Desert through Friday. \n\n* Chances of showers and thunderstorms across the region today and \n  Wednesday, with gusty winds being the greatest threat.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Monday.\nHigh pressure currently centered over Arizona and New Mexico will \ngradually shift west over the next couple of days, which will bring \nincreasing heights resulting in well above normal temperatures. \nWednesday and Thursday are expected to be the hottest days of the \nweek with most areas seeing readings about 8-10 degrees above \nnormal. An approaching trough over the eastern Pacific will help to \nsuppress the high on Friday with lowering heights and slightly \ncooler temperatures; however, temperatures will still remain several \ndegrees above normal. As a result, there remains an Extreme Heat \nWarning in effect through Friday for the Mojave Desert. While \nafternoon high temperatures have been this hot (or hotter) earlier \nin the summer without heat headlines, it is important to note that \nthere is historically a resurgence of heat-related impacts at the \nend of the season. See the CLIMATE section at the bottom for daily \nrecords. \n\nPWATs are still ranging around 1.00 inch across the Mojave Desert \nand near 0.75 inches over the southern Great Basin, but with weak \ninstability, storms on Tuesday were rather weak and very isolated. \nIt looks like another similar day today with the moisture in place, \nbut weak instability. We can't completely rule out an isolated \nstorm, but these should generally remain isolated and confined to \nthe higher terrain. Thursday is a bit more interesting as the \napproaching trough is forecast to help bring a resurgence of at \nleast mid level moisture into the region along with continued weak \ninstability. The latest 00Z HRRR is indicating more scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms with the primary threat being gusty and \nerratic winds as the latest forecasted soundings are showing a \nstrong inverted V. It is also possible that we may see too much mid \nlevel cloud cover to really get convection going. By Friday into the \nweekend, the trough is expected to bring much drier air into the \nregion with thunderstorm chances ending across the forecast area. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Light\nand variable winds will continue through late morning when winds \nwill settle from the east. Winds will gradually veer throughout \nthe morning hours, becoming more southeasterly later this \nafternoon. Winds will swing to the southwest after sunset in a \ntypical diurnal fashion. Generally dry conditions are expected \nthough some cumulus build ups are again expected across the higher\nterrain. Temperatures will exceed 100F through 17Z-04Z. VFR skies\nprevailing with increasing high clouds tonight into Thursday \nmorning. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Typical wind patterns\nare expected today with winds generally under 10KT. Southerly \nbreezes are expected in the Colorado River Valley and Owens Valley\nthis afternoon with 15 to 20 knot wind gusts expected through \nsunset. KDAG will briefly gust out of the west to around 20KT this\nevening like they usually do, but gusts should end by 09Z \ntonight. Isolated convection will be possible in the higher \nterrain of the southern Great Basin and northwestern Arizona this \nafternoon. Erratic gusty outflow winds will be the primary \nconcerns with any storms that develop. Outside of areas where \nconvection develops, VFR skies will prevail through the period \nwith increasing high clouds tonight.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...Numerous climate locations have a forecast high\ntemperature and/or forecast warm low temperature within 3 degrees of \nthe daily record.\n\nThe table below shows the daily record maximum temperature and the \nyear the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which records \nare in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nMAX           WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     109(2001)*  110(2017)*  110(2019)*\nBishop        103(2019)*  103(2019)*  103(2017)\nNeedles       122(1924)   120(1891)   116(2023)*\nDaggett       111(2011)   111(2017)   111(2017)\nKingman       106(2020)*  107(2017)*  107(2009)\nDesert Rock   106(2001)*  107(2017)   107(2017)\nDeath Valley  122(2017)*  124(1924)   120(2011)*\n\nThe table below shows the daily record warm minimum temperature and \nthe year the record was last set. An asterisk (*) denotes which \nrecords are in jeopardy (within 3 degrees of the forecast).\n\nWARM MIN      WED, AUG 26 THU, AUG 27 FRI, AUG 28\n              Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)  Record(Yr)\n\nLas Vegas     88(2020)*   87(2011)*   86(2019)* \nBishop        66(1988)    67(1970)    62(1970) \nNeedles       93(2011)    93(2011)    94(2017) \nDaggett       82(1981)*   81(2011)*   82(2017)* \nKingman       79(1945)    79(2020)*   75(2019)* \nDesert Rock   78(1985)*   78(1995)*   77(2011)* \nDeath Valley  98(1991)*   95(2011)*  100(2019)* \n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Gorelow\nAVIATION...Stessman\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
